# Words 101-150
R = [
("VRB-101","catch – caught – caught – catching","'Catch' is irregular: the past is 'caught', not 'catched'.","CATCH → CAUGHT, like TEACH → TAUGHT.","'Catch a cold', 'catch a bus', 'catch you later' — catch is used far beyond balls.","Write a sentence about a time you caught something that was falling.",("the ball comes towards you","open your hands","hold it tight"),"catch vs hold","Catch = take hold of something moving. Hold = keep something in your hands."),
("VRB-102","kick – kicked – kicked – kicking","'Kick' takes a direct object, with no 'to'.","KICK sounds like the sharp sound of a foot hitting a ball.","'Kick off' = start (a match or a meeting). 'Kick the habit' = stop a bad habit.","Write a sentence about a game where you kick a ball.",("run towards the ball","swing your leg","the ball flies away"),"kick vs hit","Kick = strike with your foot. Hit = strike with your hand or an object."),
("VRB-103","roll – rolled – rolled – rolling","For a length of time, use 'for' with the present perfect continuous.","ROLL has double L, like two wheels rolling.","'Roll' also means a list of names: 'the attendance roll'. 'Roti roll' is a food.","Write a sentence about something that rolled under your bed.",("push the ball gently","it turns over and over","it stops at the wall"),"roll vs role","Roll = move by turning. Role = a part someone plays. Same sound, different meaning."),
("VRB-104","slide – slid – slid – sliding","'Slide' is irregular: the past is 'slid', not 'slided'.","SLIDE → SLID: the E slides away.","'Slide' also means one page of a presentation: 'Next slide, please.'","Write a sentence about a time you slid on a wet floor.",("step on the wet floor","move smoothly without control","stop or fall"),"slide vs slip","Slide = move smoothly, often on purpose. Slip = slide by accident and lose balance."),
("VRB-105","hop – hopped – hopped – hopping","A finished past action needs the past tense, and 'hop' doubles the p: hopped.","HOP → HOPPED, like STOP → STOPPED.","'Hop in!' is a casual way to invite someone into your car.","Write a sentence about an animal that hops.",("stand on one foot","push up","land on the same foot"),"hop vs hope","Hop (short o) = jump on one foot. Hope (long o, silent e) = wish for something. Hopped vs hoped."),
("VRB-106","skip – skipped – skipped – skipping","A finished past action needs the past tense, and 'skip' doubles the p: skipped.","SKIP → SKIPPED: double P.","'Skip a class' or 'skip a meal' means miss it on purpose.","Write a sentence about a meal you sometimes skip.",("see a long list","jump over one item","move to the next"),"skip vs miss","Skip = leave out on purpose. Miss = fail to catch, attend or notice, often by accident."),
("VRB-107","dive – dived/dove – dived – diving","A repeated action continuing from a past time to now needs the present perfect continuous with 'since'.","DIVE: D for Down, head first.","'Dive into' also means start something with energy: 'Let's dive into the topic.'","Write a sentence about a place where you would like to dive.",("stand at the edge","jump head first","enter the water"),"dive vs jump","Jump = leave the ground with your feet. Dive = go head first, usually into water."),
("VRB-108","float – floated – floated – floating","With 'since', use the present perfect continuous.","FLOAT has 'OAT' — oats float on milk.","'Float an idea' means suggest it to see how people react.","Write a sentence about something that floats on water.",("drop a leaf in the pond","it stays on top","the wind moves it slowly"),"float vs swim","Swim = move through water on purpose. Float = stay on the surface without effort."),
("VRB-109","sink – sank – sunk – sinking","'Sink' is irregular: sink – sank – sunk.","SINK → SANK → SUNK, like DRINK → DRANK → DRUNK.","A 'sink' is also the basin in a kitchen. 'It hasn't sunk in' = I haven't fully understood it yet.","Write a sentence about something that sank in water.",("the boat fills with water","it goes down","it rests at the bottom"),"sink vs drown","Things sink. Living things drown: they die from being under water."),
("VRB-110","steer – steered – steered – steering","A finished past action needs -ed: steered.","The STEERing wheel is what you use to STEER.","'Steer clear of' means avoid: 'Steer clear of junk food.'","Write a sentence about how you steer a bicycle.",("see a turn ahead","turn the wheel","the car changes direction"),"steer vs drive","Drive = operate the whole vehicle. Steer = control only the direction."),
("VRB-111","work – worked – worked – working","For a length of time up to now, use 'for' with the present perfect continuous.","WORK and WORD differ by one letter: your work speaks for you.","'Does it work?' means does it function. 'Work out' = exercise, or solve.","Write a sentence about where you work or want to work.",("reach the office","do your tasks","finish for the day"),"work vs job","Work = the activity (uncountable). Job = your paid position (countable). 'I have a job'; 'I have a lot of work.'"),
("VRB-112","study – studied – studied – studying","For a length of time up to now, use 'for' with the present perfect continuous.","STUDY → STUDIED: Y becomes I before -ed.","'Study' is also a noun for a room: 'He is in his study.'","Write a sentence about which subject you study most.",("open your books","read and make notes","close the book, feeling ready"),"study vs learn","Study = the effort (reading, practising). Learn = the result (you now know it)."),
("VRB-113","learn – learned/learnt – learned/learnt – learning","For a length of time up to now, use the present perfect continuous with 'for', not the simple continuous.","LEARN has 'EAR' inside: you learn by listening.","'Learnt' is British; 'learned' is used in both British and American English.","Write a sentence about a skill you learned recently.",("don't know a skill","practise it","can do it"),"learn vs teach","You learn from someone. Someone teaches you. Never 'He learned me.'"),
("VRB-114","read – read – read – reading","'Read' keeps the same spelling in the past, but it is pronounced 'red'.","READ (reed) today, READ (red) yesterday — like LEAD and LED in sound.","'Read out' means read aloud for others.","Write a sentence about the last book or article you read.",("open the book","follow the words","understand the story"),"read vs study","Read = look at text and understand it. Study = read carefully in order to learn."),
("VRB-115","write – wrote – written – writing","'Write' is irregular: write – wrote – written.","WRITE has a silent W, like WRONG and WRAP.","'Write down' = note it on paper. 'Write to' someone = send them a letter or email.","Write a sentence about who you last wrote a message to.",("take a pen","put words on paper","read what you wrote"),"write vs right","Write = make words. Right = correct, or the opposite of left. Same sound."),
("VRB-116","type – typed – typed – typing","With 'since', use the present perfect continuous.","TYPE: T for Tap the keys.","'Type' is also a noun meaning kind: 'What type of music do you like?'","Write a sentence about how fast you can type.",("open a document","press the keys","words appear on screen"),"type vs write","Write = with a pen, or in general. Type = using a keyboard."),
("VRB-117","print – printed – printed – printing","'Printer' already means the machine, so 'printer machine' repeats the idea.","PRINT has 'PRINT' in 'PRINTER': the machine that prints.","'Print out' and 'print' mean the same. A 'printout' is the printed page.","Write a sentence about something you printed recently.",("click Print","the printer puts ink on paper","collect the pages"),"print vs copy","Print = make a paper copy from a computer file. Copy (photocopy) = make a copy from paper."),
("VRB-118","calculate – calculated – calculated – calculating","'Calculate' takes a direct object; don't add 'about'.","CALCULATE and CALCULATOR: the machine that calculates.","'A calculated risk' is a risk you thought about carefully.","Write a sentence about something you calculate every month.",("see the numbers","add or multiply them","get the total"),"calculate vs count","Count = find how many (1, 2, 3…). Calculate = work out an answer using maths."),
("VRB-119","manage – managed – managed – managing","'Manage to' is followed by the base verb, not -ing. A finished past action also needs the past tense.","MANAGE has 'MAN' + 'AGE': it takes a grown-up to manage things.","'I'll manage' = I'll be fine on my own.","Write a sentence about a difficult task you managed to finish.",("the task looks hard","keep trying","succeed: 'I managed it!'"),"manage vs arrange","Manage = handle or succeed. Arrange = plan and organise details in advance."),
("VRB-120","organize – organized – organized – organizing","'Organize' takes a direct object; don't add 'about'.","ORGANIZE has 'ORGAN': like body organs, each part in its right place.","British spelling: organise. 'An organised person' plans ahead.","Write a sentence about how you organize your study time.",("things are messy","put them in groups and order","everything is easy to find"),"organize vs arrange","Arrange = put things in a nice order or position. Organize = plan and manage a bigger system or event."),
("VRB-121","plan – planned – planned – planning","'Plan to' is followed by the base verb, not -ing.","PLAN → PLANNED: double N before -ed.","'Plan B' means a backup option.","Write a sentence about what you plan to do this weekend.",("decide a goal","write the steps","follow the steps"),"plan vs decide","Decide = make a choice. Plan = work out how and when to do it."),
("VRB-122","prepare – prepared – prepared – preparing","Use 'prepare for' + an event. With 'since', use the present perfect continuous.","PRE-PARE: get ready BEFORE ('pre') the event.","'Prepare yourself' is used for bad news: 'Prepare yourself for a shock.'","Write a sentence about how you prepare for an exam.",("the exam is near","study and practise","feel ready"),"prepare vs prepare for","Prepare something = make it ready (prepare dinner). Prepare for something = get ready for an event (prepare for the exam)."),
("VRB-123","research – researched – researched – researching","As a noun, 'research' is usually uncountable, so no 'a'. Use 'research on' or 'into' a topic.","RE-SEARCH: search again and again.","'Do some research' is more natural than 'do a research'.","Write a sentence about something you researched before buying.",("have a question","read many sources","find the answer"),"research vs search","Search = look for one thing. Research = study a topic deeply and carefully."),
("VRB-124","review – reviewed – reviewed – reviewing","'Review' takes a direct object; don't add 'about'.","RE-VIEW: view again.","A 'review' is also an opinion about a film or product: '5-star review'.","Write a sentence about something you review before an exam.",("finish a report","read it again carefully","fix any errors"),"review vs revise","Review = look at again. Revise (UK) = study again for an exam, or change and improve something."),
("VRB-125","revise – revised – revised – revising","Use 'revise for' an exam. You revise your notes, not the exam itself.","RE-VISE: look again to improve.","In India and the UK, 'revise' = study for an exam. In the US, 'review' is used instead.","Write a sentence about how many days you need to revise for an exam.",("the exam is next week","go over your notes again","feel confident"),"revise vs review","Revise = go over again to learn or improve. Review = look at again to check."),
("VRB-126","memorize – memorized – memorized – memorizing","'Memorize' takes a direct object; don't add 'about'.","MEMORIZE comes from MEMORY: put it into memory.","British spelling: memorise. 'Learn by heart' means the same.","Write a sentence about something you memorized as a child.",("read the poem","repeat it many times","say it without looking"),"memorize vs remember","Memorize = learn so you can repeat it exactly. Remember = keep or bring back to mind."),
("VRB-127","practise/practice – practised – practised – practising","'Practise' is followed by -ing, not 'to + verb'.","UK: practiCe is the noun (like adviCe), practiSe is the verb. US uses 'practice' for both.","'Practice makes perfect' — the famous saying.","Write a sentence about something you practise every day.",("try a skill","repeat it again and again","get better at it"),"practise vs practice","UK: practise (verb), practice (noun). US: practice for both. Indian textbooks usually follow UK spelling."),
("VRB-128","focus – focused – focused – focusing","Use 'focus on', not 'focus in'.","FOCUS like a camera: point it at ONE thing.","'Stay focused' is common advice before an exam or task.","Write a sentence about what helps you focus.",("switch off the phone","give all attention to one task","finish it quickly"),"focus vs concentrate","Almost the same. Focus = point attention at one thing. Concentrate = hold that attention for a long time. Both take 'on'."),
("VRB-129","concentrate – concentrated – concentrated – concentrating","Use 'concentrate on', not 'concentrate in'.","CON-CENTRE-ATE: bring everything to the centre.","'I can't concentrate' is common when it's noisy.","Write a sentence about where you concentrate best.",("sit in a quiet place","think hard about one problem","solve it"),"concentrate vs focus","Both take 'on'. Concentrate suggests longer, harder mental effort."),
("VRB-130","achieve – achieved – achieved – achieving","'Achieve' takes a noun object (a goal, a result), not 'to + verb'.","ACHIEVE: 'I before E, except after C' — achIEve.","'Achievement' is the noun: 'Getting this job is a big achievement.'","Write a sentence about a goal you achieved this year.",("set a goal","work hard for months","reach it"),"achieve vs get","Get = receive or obtain (easy). Achieve = reach something through effort.","I achieved to become a doctor.","I achieved my goal of becoming a doctor."),
("VRB-131","complete – completed – completed – completing","A finished past time like 'yesterday' needs the simple past, not the present perfect.","COMPLETE: every piece is there, nothing missing.","'Complete the form' is standard on official forms.","Write a sentence about a course or project you completed.",("start a task","work step by step","nothing is left to do"),"complete vs finish","Finish = reach the end. Complete = finish with every part done. Forms are completed; races are finished.","I have completed my homework yesterday.","I completed my homework yesterday."),
("VRB-132","finish – finished – finished – finishing","'Finish' is followed by -ing, not 'to + verb'.","FINISH has 'FIN', like the French word for 'the end'.","'Are you finished?' / 'Have you finished?' — both common.","Write a sentence about what you finished doing last night.",("start reading","read the last page","close the book"),"finish vs end","You finish an activity (finish work). Something ends by itself (the film ended)."),
("VRB-133","start – started – started – starting","He/she/it in the simple present needs -s: starts.","START and STOP: the two ends of every task.","'Start' also works for machines: 'The car won't start.'","Write a sentence about what time your day starts.",("everything is ready","press go","the activity is running"),"start vs begin","Almost the same. Start is more common in speech and is used for machines. Begin sounds slightly more formal."),
("VRB-134","begin – began – begun – beginning","He/she/it in the simple present needs -s: begins.","BEGIN → BEGAN → BEGUN, like SWIM → SWAM → SWUM.","Spelling: beginNING has double N.","Write a sentence about when your school or office year begins.",("wait for the time","the first step happens","the event is underway"),"begin vs start","Begin is slightly more formal. Machines 'start', they don't 'begin'."),
("VRB-135","apply – applied – applied – applying","Use 'apply for' a job and 'apply to' a company.","APPLY → APPLIED: Y becomes I before -ed.","'Apply' also means put on: 'Apply cream to the skin.'","Write a sentence about a job or course you applied for.",("see a job opening","send the form","wait for a reply"),"apply for vs apply to","Apply FOR the job or visa. Apply TO the company or college."),
("VRB-136","submit – submitted – submitted – submitting","Use 'submit something to someone', not 'in'.","SUBMIT → SUBMITTED: double T before -ed.","'Submit' is the button on online forms.","Write a sentence about something you submitted online recently.",("finish the form","click Submit","get a confirmation"),"submit vs send","Send = post or email anything. Submit = hand in something formally for approval or marking."),
("VRB-137","hire – hired – hired – hiring","'Hire' takes the person directly, with no 'to'.","HIRE sounds like HIGHER: a company hires people to go higher.","In India and the UK, 'hire' also means rent: 'hire a car'.","Write a sentence about who you would hire to fix your phone.",("need a worker","interview people","give one of them the job"),"hire vs employ","Hire = give someone a job (the moment). Employ = keep someone working for you (ongoing)."),
("VRB-138","fire – fired – fired – firing","The passive needs the past participle 'fired': 'was fired'.","FIRE someone: they are burnt out of the job.","'You're fired!' is informal. Formal: 'dismissed' or 'let go'.","Write a sentence about why someone might be fired from a job.",("an employee breaks rules","the boss takes action","the employee loses the job"),"fire vs resign","Fire = the employer ends your job. Resign = you choose to leave."),
("VRB-139","train – trained – trained – training","The present continuous needs -ing (training), not the past participle.","TRAIN a team like a TRAIN: one coach pulling many carriages.","'Train' also means practise for sport: 'She trains every morning.'","Write a sentence about a skill someone trained you in.",("new staff can't use the software","show and practise with them","they can do it alone"),"train vs teach","Teach = give knowledge (subjects). Train = build practical skills through practice."),
("VRB-140","supervise – supervised – supervised – supervising","'Supervise' takes a direct object; don't add 'about'.","SUPER-VISE: 'super' (above) + 'vise' (see): see from above.","A 'supervisor' is a person who checks others' work.","Write a sentence about who supervises your work or studies.",("staff start working","watch and guide them","the work is done correctly"),"supervise vs manage","Supervise = watch over daily work. Manage = be responsible for people, plans and results."),
("VRB-141","delegate – delegated – delegated – delegating","The order is 'delegate + task + to + person'. 'Last week' also needs the past tense.","DELEGATE: give a LEG of the work to someone else.","A 'delegate' (noun) is a person sent to represent a group at a meeting.","Write a sentence about a task you would delegate to a teammate.",("have too many tasks","give one task to a colleague","focus on the important one"),"delegate vs assign","Assign = give someone a task. Delegate = give away a task that was your own responsibility."),
("VRB-142","negotiate – negotiated – negotiated – negotiating","'Negotiate' takes the deal as its direct object; don't add 'about'.","NEGOTIATE: 'NEGO' sounds like 'go' back and forth until you agree.","In markets: 'Can we negotiate the price?' is a polite way to bargain.","Write a sentence about something you negotiated a price for.",("the price is high","offer and counter-offer","agree on a price"),"negotiate vs bargain","Bargain = argue about price, usually in a market. Negotiate = discuss formally to reach any agreement."),
("VRB-143","present – presented – presented – presenting","After 'will', use the base verb.","The verb is pre-SENT (stress on the 2nd syllable); the noun is PRE-sent.","'Present' also means give formally: 'present an award'.","Write a sentence about a topic you would like to present.",("prepare slides","stand and explain to the group","answer questions"),"present vs presentation","Present is the verb (I present). Presentation is the noun (my presentation)."),
("VRB-144","brainstorm – brainstormed – brainstormed – brainstorming","'Brainstorm' can take ideas as a direct object; 'about for' is not needed.","BRAIN + STORM: a storm of ideas in the brain.","'Let's brainstorm' is common in offices and classrooms.","Write a sentence about a problem your team could brainstorm.",("have a problem","share many ideas quickly","choose the best one"),"brainstorm vs discuss","Discuss = talk about a topic. Brainstorm = produce many ideas fast, without judging them."),
("VRB-145","schedule – scheduled – scheduled – scheduling","Use 'schedule something for' a day or time, not 'in'.","SCHEDULE: UK says 'SHED-yool', US says 'SKED-jool'. Both are correct.","'Ahead of schedule' = early. 'Behind schedule' = late.","Write a sentence about a meeting or class you have scheduled.",("check everyone's time","pick a day and time","put it in the calendar"),"schedule vs plan","Plan = decide what to do. Schedule = fix exactly when it happens."),
("VRB-146","meet – met – met – meeting","'Meet' is irregular: the past is 'met', not 'meeted'.","MEET → MET: drop one E.","'Nice to meet you' for the first time; 'Nice to see you' after that.","Write a sentence about where you met your best friend.",("plan a time and place","arrive and see each other","talk together"),"meet vs see","Meet = come together, often by plan or for the first time. See = notice with your eyes, or visit someone you know."),
("VRB-147","greet – greeted – greeted – greeting","'Greet' takes the person directly, with no 'to'.","GREET with a GREAT smile.","'Season's greetings' is used on festival cards.","Write a sentence about how people greet each other in your family.",("a guest arrives","say hello and smile","welcome them inside"),"greet vs welcome","Greet = say hello. Welcome = greet warmly and make someone feel at home."),
("VRB-148","invite – invited – invited – inviting","'Invite' takes the person directly, with no 'to' before them. Use 'invite someone to/for' an event.","INVITE: you send an INVITation.","'Invite' as a noun ('Thanks for the invite') is informal; 'invitation' is standard.","Write a sentence about who you would invite to your birthday.",("plan a party","send messages to friends","they come to the party"),"invite vs call","Invite = ask someone to come to an event. Call = phone someone, or shout to get attention."),
("VRB-149","introduce – introduced – introduced – introducing","'Introduce' needs 'to' before the second person: introduce A to B.","INTRO-DUCE: an INTRO leads people into knowing each other.","'Let me introduce myself' is a polite way to start at an interview or event.","Write a sentence introducing yourself to a new classmate or colleague.",("two people don't know each other","say each person's name to the other","they shake hands"),"introduce vs present","Introduce = make people known to each other. Present = show something formally to a group."),
("VRB-150","welcome – welcomed – welcomed – welcoming","'Welcome' takes the person directly, with no 'to' before them.","WEL-COME: it's WELL that you COME.","'You're welcome' is the polite reply to 'thank you'.","Write a sentence welcoming a guest to your home.",("a guest arrives at the door","smile and say 'Welcome!'","invite them in"),"welcome vs greet","Greet = say hello. Welcome = greet warmly and make someone feel wanted."),
]
